[38;5;252m[1m## A flaw I had to fix in my own harness[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The crate is a pure [38;5;10mcdylib[0m, so cargo does [1mnot[22m build it as a dependency of an integration test. My first harness only built the [38;5;10m.so[0m when it was absent, which meant tests silently ran against a stale artifact. I caught this with a negative control: injecting a plausible [38;5;10m(b != 0)[0m bool-normalization bug into [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m produced a fully green run. After changing the harness to rebuild unconditionally, the same injected bug fails loudly ([38;5;10mdriver(2, 5, 2, 0)[0m: C [38;5;10m2 5 0 0[0m, Rust [38;5;10m2 5 1 0[0m). The bug was then reverted. Without that fix the whole suite would have been vacuous.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two other harness details worth knowing: stdout capture happens at the file-descriptor level because both libraries share the process' libc [38;5;10mstdout[0m, and it requires draining [3mboth[23m libc's buffers and the [38;5;10mLineWriter[0m behind Rust's [38;5;10mstd::io::stdout[0m that libtest prints progress through. Tests are pinned to [38;5;10mRUST_TEST_THREADS = "1"[0m via [38;5;10mtranslation/.cargo/config.toml[0m, since fd 1 is process-wide.[0m[0m
